Group A of the [[2006 FIFA World Cup]] was one of eight groups in the opening round of the tournament. The group featured four teams, competition hosts [[Germany national football team|Germany]], [[Costa Rica national football team|Costa Rica]], [[Poland national football team|Poland]] and [[Ecuador national football team|Ecuador]]. Play began on 9 June with the first game of the tournament between Germany and Costa Rica, with the former attaining a 4–2 victory in the highest scoring opening game in World Cup history since the competition introduced a single game opener. Later the same day, Poland and Ecuador faced each other, with Ecuador winning 2–0. On 14 June, Germany secured their second victory of the competition, defeating Poland 1–0 following [[Oliver Neuville]]'s [[injury time]] goal. The following day, Ecuador defeated Costa Rica 3–0 to guarantee both they and Germany would advance to the round of 16.

The final set of matches was played on 20 June 2006. Germany and Ecuador contested the top place in the group, with the hosts winning 3–0. With both sides already eliminated, Poland defeated Costa Rica 2–1 to claim their only victory of the competition. Ecuador were eliminated in the following round by [[England national football team|England]] while Germany reached the semi-finals before being defeated by eventual winners [[Italy national football team|Italy]]. Germany finished third after beating [[Portugal national football team|Portugal]] in the match for third place.

==Background==
Group A was the first of eight groups to start play at the [[2006 FIFA World Cup]] in Germany. The competition consisted of 32 competitors, split into eight groups of four teams. The sides would play each other on a [[Round-robin tournament|round-robin basis]] with the top two teams in each group advancing to the round of 16.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/newsfiles/19085.pdf |title=Regulations 2006 FIFA World Cup Germany| publisher=FIFA |accessdate=9 January 2023}}</ref>

As hosts, three-time World Cup winners [[Germany national football team|Germany]] qualified automatically for the tournament and were placed into Group A.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://fifaworldcup.yahoo.com:80/releases/uefa_draw_procedure_en.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20050407120638/http://fifaworldcup.yahoo.com/releases/uefa_draw_procedure_en.pdf |url-status=dead |archive-date=7 April 2005 |title=European Zone Draw for the Preliminary Competition |publisher=Yahoo! |access-date=24 December 2017 }}</ref> Manager [[Jürgen Klinsmann]] had been appointed in 2004, but due to qualifying as hosts, had yet to take charge of his side in a competitive fixture.<ref name="gerguide">{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2006/team_guides/4369052.stm |title=Germany Team Guide |work=BBC Sport |date=22 May 2006 |access-date=6 January 2023}}</ref> His team were considered one of the favourites to win the competition with [[bookmakers]] offering odds of around 13/2,<ref name="tga303">{{cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=H2T0ZD5S86QC&dq=2006+world+cup+odds&pg=PA303 |title=The Global Art of Soccer |publisher=CusiBoy Publishing |last=Witzig |first=Richard |year=2006 |page=303 |isbn=9780977668809}}</ref> but had received criticism in the country, notably from [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] general manager [[Uli Hoeneß]] who had labelled the side "a disaster".<ref name="gerguide"/> [[CONCACAF]] members [[Costa Rica national football team|Costa Rica]] endured a poor start to qualifying, narrowly avoiding an upset defeat against [[Cuba national football team|Cuba]] in the [[2006 FIFA World Cup qualification – CONCACAF second round|second qualifying round]],<ref name="Lewis">{{Cite book |title=World Cup soccer: Germany 2006 |last=Lewis |first=Michael |year=2006 |publisher=Moyer Bell |location=Rhode Island |edition=4th |isbn=9781559213585}}</ref>{{rp|p=285}} before appointing [[Alexandre Guimarães]] as manager midway through the stage. They qualified for the tournament after finishing third in their [[2006 FIFA World Cup qualification – CONCACAF fourth round|qualification round]], reaching the finals for the third time in their history.<ref name="crcguide">{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2006/team_guides/4369308.stm |title=Costa Rica Team Guide |work=BBC Sport |date=22 May 2006 |access-date=6 January 2023}}</ref> However, they were classed as rank outsiders to win the competition, with odds as high as 500/1 being offered.<ref name="tga303"/>

To provide an even number of teams for the playoff bracket, the two teams from the [[2006 FIFA World Cup qualification (UEFA)|UEFA qualification groups]] who had finished second in their group with the best record automatically gained qualification to the World Cup. [[Poland national football team|Poland]], who finished second in [[2006 FIFA World Cup qualification – UEFA Group 6|UEFA qualification Group 6]] behind [[England national football team|England]], were one of the teams who were eligible, along with [[Sweden men's national football team|Sweden]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/european-qualifiers/news/01ae-0f847aff7079-cae797822448-1000--trio-share-in-final-day-joy/ |title=Trio share in final-day joy |publisher=UEFA |date=13 October 2005 |accessdate=6 January 2023}}</ref> Ecuador qualified after finishing in the third of four automatic qualification places in the [[2006 FIFA World Cup qualification (CONMEBOL)|CONMEBOL qualifying group]] behind [[Brazil national football team|Brazil]] and [[Argentina national football team|Argentina]],<ref>{{cite web |url=https://uk.soccerway.com/international/south-america/wc-qualifying-south-america/2006-germany/group-stage/r1518/ |title=World Cup Qualification South America |publisher=Soccerway |accessdate=6 January 2023}}</ref><ref name="Lewis"/>{{rp|p=298}} reaching the finals for only the second time. Similar to Costa Rica, Ecuador were given low odds of around 400/1.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2006/team_guides/4369186.stm |title=Ecuador Team Guide |work=BBC Sport |date=22 May 2006 |access-date=6 January 2023}}</ref> In their preview of the tournament, ''[[The Guardian]]'' labelled Group A as the "weakest" in the competition.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2006/jun/05/worldcup2006.sport3 |title=Costa Rica |newspaper=The Guardian|date=5 June 2006 |accessdate=6 January 2023}}</ref>

